      "license": "CC BY-SA 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0",
      "artist": "Ken Lund from Reno, Nevada, USA",
      "description": "The Granite Mountains is a small mountain range in San Bernardino County, California, USA, located in the Mojave Desert. They are in the Mojave National Preserve, in the National Park Service system.\nSome of the more striking rock formations in the Mojave National Preserve lie in the Granite Mountains. These granitic rocks have eroded into unusual rounded shapes that include spires, perched boulders, and curved cliff faces.\nGranitic rocks represent the roots of ancient continental-margin volcanic systems. Most of the granitic rock in the Mojave Desert is late Mesozoic in age (80 to 180 million years old). The granites formed at depth within a volcanically active mountain range comparable in geologic setting to the Andes chain in South America.\nThe granitoids formed by the slow cooling and solidification of molten magma bodies that developed above sinking slabs of oceanic crust overridden by the edge of the continent. At least 55 or 60 million years elapsed between the crystallization of the last Mesozoic magma bodies and deposition of the youngest-preserved overlying strata.\nSeveral springs exist in the Granite Mountains, though they may not flow year round.\nen.wikipedia.org/wiki/Granite_Mountains_(California)\n\nen.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Text_of_Creative_Commons_...",
      "dateCreated": "2014-10-28 07:12",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "granite-mountains-eastern-san-bernardino-county-california-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "9qjw",
